1
00:00:02,169 --> 00:00:03,246
GORDON: <i>Previously on</i> Gotham...

2
00:00:03,270 --> 00:00:04,314
- Hello.
- What's wrong with you?

3
00:00:04,338 --> 00:00:07,308
I let him out of his cage, and
he helped me get out of mine.

4
00:00:07,374 --> 00:00:09,104
You're him. The Riddler.

5
00:00:09,176 --> 00:00:10,176
Hi, Lee.

6
00:00:11,378 --> 00:00:12,539
Butch?

7
00:00:13,347 --> 00:00:15,339
- You're alive.
- Huh?

8
00:00:15,415 --> 00:00:17,850
TABITHA: <i>Oh, Butch. I tried.</i>

9
00:00:19,086 --> 00:00:21,487
Tabby, I'm gonna find a
way to get back to the old me.

10
00:00:21,555 --> 00:00:22,555
I promise.

11
00:00:23,223 --> 00:00:24,501
GORDON: <i>Tell me about
your mother, Jerome?</i>

12
00:00:24,525 --> 00:00:25,993
- Did she have any enemies?
- No.

13
00:00:26,059 --> 00:00:27,337
And do you have
any other family?

14
00:00:27,361 --> 00:00:29,159
The circus is my family.

15
00:00:30,197 --> 00:00:34,157
JEROME: <i>Now, to free the rest of
our cronies and blow this pop stand.</i>

16
00:00:34,234 --> 00:00:36,880
BRUCE: <i>I could have killed Jerome
after he kidnapped me, and I didn't.</i>

17
00:00:36,904 --> 00:00:39,703
<i>Any harm he does
now, I share a part of it.</i>

18
00:00:40,307 --> 00:00:41,536
<i>I saw Jerome tonight.</i>

19
00:00:41,642 --> 00:00:43,762
<i>He was looking for the
name of a school, St. Ignatius.</i>

20
00:00:43,844 --> 00:00:45,403
Stay the hell away
from that madman.

21
00:00:45,512 --> 00:00:46,878
I'll see you at
St. Ignatius, Jim.

22
00:01:08,235 --> 00:01:09,362
Sir.

23
00:01:10,771 --> 00:01:11,898
Sir?

24
00:01:12,205 --> 00:01:13,571
MAN: Can I help you?

25
00:01:14,007 --> 00:01:16,977
My name is Bruce Wayne.
I'm looking for Jerome Valeska.

26
00:01:17,411 --> 00:01:18,411
Has he been here?

27
00:01:18,979 --> 00:01:21,778
MAN: I didn't have the
pleasure of meeting Mr. Valeska.

28
00:01:22,215 --> 00:01:24,616
However, I did
speak with his friend,

29
00:01:24,718 --> 00:01:26,550
the one with the top hat.

30
00:01:27,921 --> 00:01:29,719
- Jervis Tetch.
- Yes.

31
00:01:30,123 --> 00:01:31,147
That's the fellow.

32
00:01:31,725 --> 00:01:33,455
He was very polite.

33
00:01:36,263 --> 00:01:37,561
What did he want?

34
00:01:37,898 --> 00:01:40,299
MAN: The name of a student

35
00:01:40,400 --> 00:01:43,529
who graduated St.
Ignatius 10 years ago.

36
00:01:44,571 --> 00:01:45,800
Xander Wilde.

37
00:01:46,406 --> 00:01:49,001
Fine young boy. Very bright.

38
00:01:50,310 --> 00:01:52,438
Did Tetch tell you why
Jerome was looking for him?

39
00:01:53,180 --> 00:01:54,409
No.

40
00:01:54,848 --> 00:01:57,818
But he did have a message

41
00:01:58,185 --> 00:01:59,653
for you.

42
00:01:59,753 --> 00:02:00,982
(BEEPING)

43
00:02:07,661 --> 00:02:09,630
(BRAKES SCREECHING)

44
00:02:17,571 --> 00:02:20,439
Listen to me. You're being
hypnotized by Jervis Tetch.

45
00:02:20,507 --> 00:02:21,684
You don't wanna
press that button.

46
00:02:21,708 --> 00:02:22,708
But I have to.

47
00:02:23,110 --> 00:02:25,306
- He's depending on me.
- GORDON: Bruce!

48
00:02:25,879 --> 00:02:27,006
Step away from him.

49
00:02:27,180 --> 00:02:28,478
BRUCE: Please.

50
00:02:29,016 --> 00:02:31,713
- Hand me the detonator.
- MAN: I want to.

51
00:02:33,220 --> 00:02:34,654
But I can't.

52
00:02:35,122 --> 00:02:36,420
BRUCE: Captain Gordon, look.

53
00:02:36,523 --> 00:02:37,923
He's under Jervis' spell.

54
00:02:38,859 --> 00:02:39,986
Oh!

55
00:02:48,101 --> 00:02:50,002
(POCKET WATCH TICKING)

56
00:02:52,472 --> 00:02:53,472
(GRUNTS)

57
00:02:57,177 --> 00:02:58,406
Get down!

58
00:03:25,038 --> 00:03:26,038
(CLINKING)

59
00:03:26,106 --> 00:03:27,699
JEROME: Hear ye, hear ye!

60
00:03:27,774 --> 00:03:31,006
I'd like to officially welcome
you all to our first ever

61
00:03:31,244 --> 00:03:33,338
Mandatory Brunch Meeting.

62
00:03:33,780 --> 00:03:35,260
Now, I know what
you're all thinking.

63
00:03:35,782 --> 00:03:39,549
Why have I gathered
this legion of horribles?

64
00:03:40,921 --> 00:03:43,356
That has a nice ring to it.
Write that down, will ya?

65
00:03:44,591 --> 00:03:46,992
Well, back when I was in Arkham,

66
00:03:47,394 --> 00:03:51,695
I came up with a plan to
turn this city into a madhouse.

67
00:03:52,032 --> 00:03:55,730
Now I am on the cusp
of making that happen,

68
00:03:55,802 --> 00:03:58,294
but I need help from all of...

69
00:03:58,405 --> 00:04:00,738
Put all questions
on ice until the end.

70
00:04:01,074 --> 00:04:02,201
<i>Danke schön.</i>

71
00:04:02,909 --> 00:04:04,138
Mr. Oswald.

72
00:04:04,578 --> 00:04:07,138
Thank you for doing your
part in hosting us today.

73
00:04:07,781 --> 00:04:09,409
Anything for an old friend.

74
00:04:09,616 --> 00:04:10,709
Crane.

75
00:04:10,784 --> 00:04:12,116
Where are we at with the...

76
00:04:12,452 --> 00:04:13,715
You know what.

77
00:04:14,354 --> 00:04:16,516
It's coming along dreadfully.

78
00:04:16,590 --> 00:04:19,082
Oh, Pun-worthy. Excellent.

79
00:04:19,192 --> 00:04:22,390
Freeze, Ice Man,
you're our science guy.

80
00:04:22,896 --> 00:04:24,421
We're gonna need a lab.

81
00:04:24,631 --> 00:04:25,724
A big one.

82
00:04:26,566 --> 00:04:27,659
Easy enough.

83
00:04:27,768 --> 00:04:31,136
Mmm. Now, all we need
is Mr. Tetch to return

84
00:04:31,238 --> 00:04:33,070
with some very
important information.

85
00:04:34,141 --> 00:04:36,269
Use the tongs, Carl.

86
00:04:38,411 --> 00:04:39,538
You still haven't told us

87
00:04:39,613 --> 00:04:42,310
how you're going to
take over the underworld.

88
00:04:43,416 --> 00:04:45,112
Who says I wanna do that?

89
00:04:45,485 --> 00:04:46,748
I'm an artist.

90
00:04:46,953 --> 00:04:49,718
I just wanna paint
the town crazy.

91
00:04:50,390 --> 00:04:55,294
Once that happens, I could care
less how you horribles rip the city apart.

92
00:04:56,663 --> 00:04:58,928
I'm in. Let's do this.

93
00:04:59,132 --> 00:05:01,192
Well, not yet.

94
00:05:01,501 --> 00:05:06,201
Nothing can happen until we have
our one last essential ingredient.

95
00:05:06,807 --> 00:05:08,366
(DOOR CLOSES)

96
00:05:10,744 --> 00:05:14,181
The one thing that'll
tie it all together.

97
00:05:21,288 --> 00:05:22,620
Who is Xander Wilde?

98
00:05:24,191 --> 00:05:25,191
I don't know.

99
00:05:25,292 --> 00:05:27,352
Did Jerome say anything
at his uncle's diner?

100
00:05:27,894 --> 00:05:30,489
Anything that could tell us
why he's going after him?

101
00:05:30,564 --> 00:05:31,974
If I knew that, Captain,
I would tell you.

102
00:05:31,998 --> 00:05:32,998
Would you?

103
00:05:33,867 --> 00:05:35,460
How can I trust
anything you say?

104
00:05:35,535 --> 00:05:37,731
I told you not to come
here and you did anyway.

105
00:05:37,838 --> 00:05:39,015
Damn it, you could
have been killed.

106
00:05:39,039 --> 00:05:41,039
All right, Captain, I think
you've made your point.

107
00:05:41,074 --> 00:05:42,201
And you?

108
00:05:42,309 --> 00:05:44,005
Did you know he was
going after Jerome?

109
00:05:45,212 --> 00:05:46,771
Indeed, I did, yes.

110
00:05:47,380 --> 00:05:49,542
If he gets involved
in this case again,

111
00:05:49,649 --> 00:05:51,379
I'll press charges. We clear?

112
00:05:53,887 --> 00:05:54,980
Come on, son.

113
00:05:56,723 --> 00:05:57,850
HARVEY: Yo!

114
00:05:59,226 --> 00:06:00,819
I just talked to
an administrator.

115
00:06:00,894 --> 00:06:03,386
Xander Wilde was some
kind of child prodigy.

116
00:06:03,496 --> 00:06:06,398
Now he's a hotshot structural
engineer at Meyer and Hayes.

117
00:06:06,900 --> 00:06:08,596
What does Jerome
want with an engineer?

118
00:06:08,702 --> 00:06:10,398
Maybe he wants him
to build him something.

119
00:06:12,572 --> 00:06:13,835
Let's go.

120
00:06:17,410 --> 00:06:19,311
(MUZAK PLAYING)

121
00:06:21,348 --> 00:06:23,214
(WHISTLING)

122
00:06:24,684 --> 00:06:26,175
(BLOWING)

123
00:06:28,521 --> 00:06:30,217
I used to be a great whistler.

124
00:06:30,523 --> 00:06:31,889
Mmm-hmm.

125
00:06:34,794 --> 00:06:36,592
Um, yeah, hi.

126
00:06:36,696 --> 00:06:39,063
I'm here to see
Xander Wilde. And, no...

127
00:06:39,633 --> 00:06:41,465
I don't have an appointment.

128
00:06:43,403 --> 00:06:44,496
(WHIMPERS)

129
00:06:44,671 --> 00:06:47,402
Don't bother going to
security. They're all...

130
00:06:47,507 --> 00:06:49,567
- (RECEPTIONIST SCREAMING)
- Headless.

131
00:06:55,015 --> 00:06:57,712
(INDISTINCT CHATTER)

132
00:07:00,987 --> 00:07:03,286
All right. If you would all
please look at the rendering,

133
00:07:03,390 --> 00:07:06,588
you'll notice that there are several
interesting engineering problems

134
00:07:06,660 --> 00:07:08,700
- that present themselves. Mostly...
- (GUN COCKING)

135
00:07:09,062 --> 00:07:10,291
(PEOPLE GASPING)

136
00:07:11,097 --> 00:07:12,656
Oh, don't get up.

137
00:07:12,766 --> 00:07:14,735
I won't waste too
much of your time.

138
00:07:14,901 --> 00:07:16,096
(OBJECT SHATTERING)

139
00:07:17,470 --> 00:07:19,701
I've got a business proposition.

140
00:07:20,040 --> 00:07:22,771
Tell me what I want to know
and you don't get turned inside out.

141
00:07:22,876 --> 00:07:24,708
Please, don't...
Don't hurt anyone.

142
00:07:24,778 --> 00:07:28,408
Where is Xander Wilde?

143
00:07:29,049 --> 00:07:31,280
- He's... He's not here.
- Yeah.

144
00:07:31,651 --> 00:07:34,120
I'm not blind. Maybe you are.

145
00:07:35,956 --> 00:07:37,015
Um, you know? Hmm?

146
00:07:37,123 --> 00:07:40,355
No one does. He's
never set foot in this office.

147
00:07:40,460 --> 00:07:41,621
- Okay.
- (GROANS)

148
00:07:41,928 --> 00:07:43,521
- (ALL GASP)
- Oh, God!

149
00:07:43,797 --> 00:07:45,459
(BREATHING HEAVILY)

150
00:07:45,966 --> 00:07:47,764
(STAMMERING) We've never met.

151
00:07:47,834 --> 00:07:49,894
I've only ever
communicated with him

152
00:07:50,603 --> 00:07:51,662
through a proxy.

153
00:07:52,906 --> 00:07:54,499
- Proxy?
- Yeah.

154
00:07:55,942 --> 00:07:56,942
(LAUGHING)

155
00:07:57,010 --> 00:08:00,674
That slippery little sucker
thinks he can get away from me.

156
00:08:00,780 --> 00:08:01,780
(GROANS)

157
00:08:01,881 --> 00:08:04,851
Where can I find
this proxy, pray tell?

158
00:08:04,951 --> 00:08:07,216
(STAMMERING)

159
00:08:07,320 --> 00:08:08,879
I'll tell you. I'll tell you.

160
00:08:08,989 --> 00:08:10,366
- (ELEVATOR BELL DINGS)
- (PANICKED CLAMORING)

161
00:08:10,390 --> 00:08:11,881
Don't take the elevator!

162
00:08:12,125 --> 00:08:14,492
(PEOPLE SCREAMING)

163
00:08:16,963 --> 00:08:18,591
Jimbo!

164
00:08:18,665 --> 00:08:21,032
Old buddy, old pal,
it's great to see ya!

165
00:08:21,534 --> 00:08:22,763
Drop the gun.

166
00:08:22,836 --> 00:08:24,634
You are such a stick in the mud.

167
00:08:24,704 --> 00:08:25,967
Give it up, clown boy!

168
00:08:27,173 --> 00:08:28,197
Hey.

169
00:08:29,476 --> 00:08:30,944
Knock, knock.

170
00:08:33,513 --> 00:08:35,209
You were supposed
to say, "Who's there?"

171
00:08:41,855 --> 00:08:44,051
- Thank you, Francis.
- Take down their statements.

172
00:08:45,025 --> 00:08:46,721
Hey, Jim. Same story all around.

173
00:08:46,826 --> 00:08:48,692
No one knows Xander.
No one even met him.

174
00:08:48,995 --> 00:08:50,395
Apparently, he had a proxy

175
00:08:50,497 --> 00:08:52,591
who handled his
dealings with the company.

176
00:08:53,199 --> 00:08:55,725
Hayes was the only one
who knew their identity,

177
00:08:55,835 --> 00:08:59,033
but obviously, he's not talking.

178
00:08:59,339 --> 00:09:01,865
So, we have to figure out
another way to find Xander.

179
00:09:02,409 --> 00:09:03,968
I'm ahead of you.
We looked into it.

180
00:09:04,144 --> 00:09:07,581
No driver's license. No known
addresses. Guy's a ghost.

181
00:09:08,148 --> 00:09:10,028
Everything that's happened
in the last 24 hours,

182
00:09:10,083 --> 00:09:11,551
the breakout, Tetch,

183
00:09:11,651 --> 00:09:13,643
it's all been about
Jerome finding this guy.

184
00:09:13,720 --> 00:09:16,032
I don't think it has anything to
do with him being an engineer.

185
00:09:16,056 --> 00:09:17,649
- This seems personal.
- Makes sense.

186
00:09:17,724 --> 00:09:19,693
I mean, Jerome went to
his uncle to look for him.

187
00:09:20,393 --> 00:09:21,986
Yeah, so how are they connected?

188
00:09:24,097 --> 00:09:25,097
What's up?

189
00:09:26,332 --> 00:09:27,595
Is that Wayne Plaza?

190
00:09:28,201 --> 00:09:30,261
Take a look at the
initials of the engineer.

191
00:09:32,238 --> 00:09:33,934
- "X.W." Xander Wilde.
- Uh-huh.

192
00:09:34,774 --> 00:09:36,734
Looks like we're gonna
have to go talk to the kid.

193
00:09:37,410 --> 00:09:38,776
He's gonna be
thrilled to see you.

194
00:10:02,001 --> 00:10:03,435
What time is it?

195
00:10:03,603 --> 00:10:05,504
ALL: Riddle time!

196
00:10:05,605 --> 00:10:06,629
(ROCK MUSIC PLAYING)

197
00:10:06,739 --> 00:10:08,367
What time is it?

198
00:10:08,441 --> 00:10:10,569
ALL: Riddle time!

199
00:10:10,944 --> 00:10:14,210
That's right! It's riddle time

200
00:10:14,280 --> 00:10:16,579
- at the Riddle Factory!
- ALL: The Riddle Factory!

201
00:10:16,649 --> 00:10:19,551
(ALL CHEERING)

202
00:10:25,959 --> 00:10:28,326
Our first contestant is...

203
00:10:29,095 --> 00:10:30,427
Lars.

204
00:10:30,763 --> 00:10:32,891
(IN GERMAN ACCENT) From
the now-defunct biker gang,

205
00:10:33,299 --> 00:10:34,790
The Street Demonz.

206
00:10:35,969 --> 00:10:38,247
(IN NORMAL VOICE) It's been
a rough go of it lately, huh, Lars?

207
00:10:38,271 --> 00:10:39,967
I've fallen on hard times.

208
00:10:41,774 --> 00:10:44,209
But I'm damn happy to
be here with you, Riddler.

209
00:10:44,611 --> 00:10:46,102
That's the narrow spirit I love.

210
00:10:46,679 --> 00:10:47,806
Now, you know the rules.

211
00:10:47,881 --> 00:10:51,340
I ask a riddle, you answer
correctly, you ask me one.

212
00:10:51,451 --> 00:10:52,612
You win 'em both,

213
00:10:53,119 --> 00:10:56,385
and you're walking out of
here with cold hard cash!

214
00:10:56,489 --> 00:10:58,981
(CROWD CHEERING)

215
00:11:00,627 --> 00:11:01,788
Lila.

216
00:11:02,195 --> 00:11:03,527
Prepare the timer.

217
00:11:17,877 --> 00:11:23,976
I am heavy forward, but
backward I am not. What am I?

218
00:11:25,018 --> 00:11:26,145
(CREAKS)

219
00:11:31,558 --> 00:11:33,618
Okay. I'm heavy forward...

220
00:11:34,661 --> 00:11:36,789
But backward I'm not.

221
00:11:39,132 --> 00:11:41,260
Okay, wait... Wait a minute.

222
00:11:41,901 --> 00:11:43,335
No, no, I know this.

223
00:11:43,937 --> 00:11:45,371
It's...

224
00:11:45,872 --> 00:11:47,340
No, no, I know this. Um...

225
00:11:49,175 --> 00:11:50,905
Wait, wait, wait.

226
00:11:54,581 --> 00:11:55,913
It's the hour glass.

227
00:12:02,255 --> 00:12:04,918
(BUZZER SOUNDING)

228
00:12:05,024 --> 00:12:08,620
The answer is, "Ton." T-O-N.

229
00:12:08,895 --> 00:12:11,990
Backwards I am, "Not."

230
00:12:12,398 --> 00:12:14,094
Sorry, Lars.

231
00:12:17,503 --> 00:12:19,404
ALL: Spin the wheel!

232
00:12:19,505 --> 00:12:20,905
Spin the wheel!

233
00:12:21,341 --> 00:12:23,276
Spin the wheel!

234
00:12:23,376 --> 00:12:24,867
Spin the wheel!

235
00:12:25,178 --> 00:12:26,703
You hear them, Lars.

236
00:12:27,347 --> 00:12:32,911
It's time to spin the
wheel of misfortune!

237
00:12:34,354 --> 00:12:36,050
ALL: Spin the wheel!

238
00:12:36,122 --> 00:12:37,750
Spin the wheel!

239
00:12:37,857 --> 00:12:39,189
Spin the wheel!

240
00:12:39,259 --> 00:12:40,727
Lila, please.

241
00:12:41,094 --> 00:12:42,460
ALL: Spin the wheel!

242
00:12:42,562 --> 00:12:44,224
Spin the wheel!

243
00:12:44,297 --> 00:12:45,890
Spin the wheel!

244
00:12:45,965 --> 00:12:47,490
Spin the wheel!

245
00:12:47,600 --> 00:12:49,125
Spin the wheel!

246
00:12:50,169 --> 00:12:52,604
(BELL DINGING)

247
00:12:52,739 --> 00:12:54,332
My personal favorite.

248
00:12:54,440 --> 00:12:56,568
Rabid sack of rats.

249
00:12:56,676 --> 00:12:58,269
(PEOPLE LAUGHING)

250
00:12:58,344 --> 00:12:59,344
Yeah!

251
00:13:01,748 --> 00:13:04,616
- (RATS SQUEAKING)
- LEE: All right, that's enough. Enough!

252
00:13:07,453 --> 00:13:08,944
NYGMA: Doc Thompkins.

253
00:13:09,255 --> 00:13:12,123
What brings Your Majesty
down to our rabble?

254
00:13:12,191 --> 00:13:13,454
How are you doing, Ed?

255
00:13:14,160 --> 00:13:16,755
Haven't seen you since
we put Oswald on ice.

256
00:13:17,664 --> 00:13:19,997
Well, I'm doing far
better than Sofia Falcone,

257
00:13:20,099 --> 00:13:21,226
from what I understand.

258
00:13:21,301 --> 00:13:24,100
Whatever the hell
this is, it's over.

259
00:13:25,138 --> 00:13:26,629
I don't think so.

260
00:13:27,140 --> 00:13:28,403
Ladies and gentlemen,

261
00:13:29,642 --> 00:13:31,975
our queen has just informed me

262
00:13:32,445 --> 00:13:34,812
she wants to shut us down.
How do you feel about that?

263
00:13:34,914 --> 00:13:37,247
(CROWD BOOING)

264
00:13:41,421 --> 00:13:42,582
You hear that?

265
00:13:43,489 --> 00:13:45,617
That's the sound of democracy.

266
00:13:45,692 --> 00:13:48,287
The people have spoken.

267
00:13:49,662 --> 00:13:53,190
And my name is The Riddler.

268
00:13:55,835 --> 00:13:56,835
Okay.

269
00:14:00,807 --> 00:14:02,469
You wanna be entertained?

270
00:14:03,076 --> 00:14:04,203
Hold this, will you?

271
00:14:07,714 --> 00:14:09,307
- Then I'll beat him.
- CROWD: Oh!

272
00:14:10,683 --> 00:14:12,083
You can't be serious.

273
00:14:12,819 --> 00:14:15,414
You realize what
happens when you lose.

274
00:14:15,521 --> 00:14:17,456
I know. I spin the wheel.

275
00:14:18,124 --> 00:14:21,060
But if I win, you
shut this place down.

276
00:14:22,795 --> 00:14:25,526
(CROWD JEERING)

277
00:14:28,868 --> 00:14:30,860
We have our next contestant!

278
00:14:30,970 --> 00:14:33,701
(CROWD CHEERING)

279
00:14:52,425 --> 00:14:54,621
JEROME: Got any
ice cream in there?

280
00:14:57,730 --> 00:15:01,258
I've always had a soft
spot for Chunky Monkey.

281
00:15:02,235 --> 00:15:04,261
I was wondering
when you would come.

282
00:15:04,737 --> 00:15:07,571
Me? That's very flattering.

283
00:15:08,074 --> 00:15:12,535
So, tell me, Little Miss
Proxy, where is he?

284
00:15:13,746 --> 00:15:15,442
I'll never tell you.

285
00:15:18,351 --> 00:15:19,351
Hmm,

286
00:15:20,420 --> 00:15:21,752
I don't know.

287
00:15:23,523 --> 00:15:24,786
See...

288
00:15:25,792 --> 00:15:28,193
I can be very persuasive.

289
00:15:30,263 --> 00:15:31,390
See that?

290
00:15:32,899 --> 00:15:35,095
Yeah. I was
wondering about that.

291
00:15:35,201 --> 00:15:38,365
You got, like, a big
dog or something?

292
00:15:38,438 --> 00:15:39,633
No.

293
00:15:39,739 --> 00:15:41,901
That's how I'm going
to transport you.

294
00:15:42,208 --> 00:15:43,208
Hmm?

295
00:15:43,976 --> 00:15:45,274
(JEROME GROANS)

296
00:15:52,452 --> 00:15:53,977
I've got him.

297
00:16:12,271 --> 00:16:13,466
Butch?

298
00:16:24,450 --> 00:16:26,112
I had heard you were different.

299
00:16:27,787 --> 00:16:29,449
But I will be honest...

300
00:16:30,623 --> 00:16:31,623
I like it.

301
00:16:33,092 --> 00:16:34,253
What do you want?

302
00:16:36,362 --> 00:16:38,661
I assume you've heard
about the Arkham breakout.

303
00:16:40,266 --> 00:16:44,636
Well, I just came from
brunch with Jerome Valeska,

304
00:16:45,671 --> 00:16:48,835
and let's just say that
he has plans for Gotham.

305
00:16:50,476 --> 00:16:51,671
What kind of plans?

306
00:16:53,012 --> 00:16:54,981
That part wasn't entirely clear.

307
00:16:55,815 --> 00:16:58,148
Seemed to involve
a great deal of paint.

308
00:16:58,851 --> 00:16:59,851
No matter.

309
00:16:59,919 --> 00:17:04,254
I have seen his kind come and
go. Jerome Valeska will flame out.

310
00:17:05,324 --> 00:17:06,383
And in the rubble,

311
00:17:07,059 --> 00:17:08,891
I will reclaim the underworld.

312
00:17:12,098 --> 00:17:14,192
I could use a good
man like you by my side.

313
00:17:14,267 --> 00:17:15,599
(CHUCKLES)

314
00:17:16,102 --> 00:17:18,537
You must be all out of
allies to come looking for me.

315
00:17:19,038 --> 00:17:20,038
Don't be modest.

316
00:17:20,740 --> 00:17:22,709
With your brute
strength and my brains,

317
00:17:22,808 --> 00:17:24,037
we will be unstoppable.

318
00:17:24,143 --> 00:17:25,167
I did that job.

319
00:17:25,578 --> 00:17:27,877
- I'm my own man now.
- (PENGUIN LAUGHS)

320
00:17:28,347 --> 00:17:29,406
Really?

321
00:17:31,217 --> 00:17:34,517
Because from where I am, you
look like a man who has nothing.

322
00:17:34,587 --> 00:17:36,818
You'd be wise to watch
what you say to me.

323
00:17:37,356 --> 00:17:40,224
- I'm not the same man you remember.
- And neither am I.

324
00:17:40,560 --> 00:17:41,892
Look at the two of us.

325
00:17:42,261 --> 00:17:44,696
We used to run
this town. Together.

326
00:17:45,231 --> 00:17:46,324
We were friends.

327
00:17:46,999 --> 00:17:49,434
Look, you want your
old life back, so do I.

328
00:17:50,403 --> 00:17:51,837
- We are the same.
- Wrong!

329
00:17:51,904 --> 00:17:53,805
You can have your old life back.

330
00:17:53,906 --> 00:17:55,602
I can't. Look at me.

331
00:17:55,708 --> 00:17:57,768
Tabitha will never
love me again.

332
00:17:58,444 --> 00:18:01,073
Slaughter Swamp
turned me into a monster!

333
00:18:02,148 --> 00:18:03,446
Now get out of here.

334
00:18:03,983 --> 00:18:05,417
If I ever see you again,

335
00:18:05,751 --> 00:18:07,617
I'll pop your head
off like a flower.

336
00:18:09,121 --> 00:18:10,453
(COUGHS)

337
00:18:11,624 --> 00:18:12,990
(DOOR OPENS)

338
00:18:13,759 --> 00:18:15,091
Let me get this straight.

339
00:18:15,227 --> 00:18:16,972
You tell us to stay out
of your investigation

340
00:18:16,996 --> 00:18:18,624
and now you want our help?

341
00:18:18,731 --> 00:18:20,461
- Subtle.
- We have reason to believe

342
00:18:20,566 --> 00:18:22,159
your father met
with Xander Wilde.

343
00:18:22,268 --> 00:18:24,379
Well, if he did, he certainly
would have made a note of it.

344
00:18:24,403 --> 00:18:25,747
What was the name
of that building again?

345
00:18:25,771 --> 00:18:26,771
Wayne Plaza.

346
00:18:27,640 --> 00:18:29,973
He was a meticulous
note taker, Thomas.

347
00:18:30,443 --> 00:18:32,241
He organized
everything by project.

348
00:18:32,778 --> 00:18:34,440
Let's see. Wayne Gardens...

349
00:18:34,747 --> 00:18:36,079
Wayne Hospital...

350
00:18:36,148 --> 00:18:39,812
Aha, here we go, Wayne Plaza.

351
00:18:44,056 --> 00:18:45,285
All right.

352
00:18:47,893 --> 00:18:49,953
Why, you're bang on
the money there, Captain.

353
00:18:50,296 --> 00:18:53,323
Xander Wilde. Says he went
to meet him to finalize the plans.

354
00:18:53,432 --> 00:18:56,368
No address, just the name
of the street, Ainslie Drive.

355
00:18:56,836 --> 00:18:58,930
It's out past Buxton
by the old rock quarry.

356
00:18:59,305 --> 00:19:00,816
BRUCE: This man
must've been very important

357
00:19:00,840 --> 00:19:03,605
if my father went out of
his way to meet him alone.

358
00:19:03,809 --> 00:19:06,802
I'm starting to think he isn't just
some recluse hiding from the world.

359
00:19:07,279 --> 00:19:08,338
He's hiding from Jerome.

360
00:19:30,703 --> 00:19:33,104
(LAUGHING)

361
00:19:35,441 --> 00:19:37,637
How interesting.

362
00:19:45,451 --> 00:19:47,147
(GRUNTS)

363
00:19:49,855 --> 00:19:52,689
Oh! Oh,

364
00:19:52,792 --> 00:19:55,227
you are incorrigible.

365
00:19:55,494 --> 00:19:57,053
And then again,

366
00:19:57,163 --> 00:19:59,029
you always were.

367
00:19:59,231 --> 00:20:00,494
(LAUGHS)

368
00:20:00,900 --> 00:20:03,529
Wait. Or was that me?

369
00:20:03,602 --> 00:20:05,503
Sometimes I get
so confused, I...

370
00:20:05,771 --> 00:20:06,898
I can't remember.

371
00:20:08,774 --> 00:20:10,504
(LAUGHS MANIACALLY)

372
00:20:13,045 --> 00:20:15,071
You didn't have to
run away like that.

373
00:20:15,414 --> 00:20:16,575
You see,

374
00:20:17,016 --> 00:20:19,451
<i>I would never hurt you,</i>

375
00:20:19,785 --> 00:20:21,083
<i>Xander.</i>

376
00:20:22,221 --> 00:20:24,190
I love you.

377
00:20:24,256 --> 00:20:25,918
(LAUGHS MANIACALLY)

378
00:20:30,563 --> 00:20:33,431
<i>I can't wait to see you.
Did you think about me?</i>

379
00:20:33,866 --> 00:20:35,494
I know that you did.

380
00:20:35,568 --> 00:20:38,037
- I'm gonna squeeze you until you pop.
- (BEEPING)

381
00:20:58,357 --> 00:20:59,757
Where the hell is the house?

382
00:21:12,772 --> 00:21:14,001
(DOORBELL BUZZING)

383
00:21:16,609 --> 00:21:17,975
James Gordon.

384
00:21:18,611 --> 00:21:20,136
<i>Here to see Xander Wilde.</i>

385
00:21:38,664 --> 00:21:41,133
So this whole
joint's underground?

386
00:21:41,500 --> 00:21:43,560
Mr. Wilde values his privacy.

387
00:21:44,003 --> 00:21:45,083
How long has he lived here?

388
00:21:45,571 --> 00:21:47,563
He finished construction
six years ago.

389
00:21:56,081 --> 00:21:58,482
- Echo, 496.
<i>- Welcome, Miss Echo.</i>

390
00:21:58,584 --> 00:21:59,916
He'll see you now.

391
00:22:09,128 --> 00:22:11,222
Thank you for
seeing us, Mr. Wilde.

392
00:22:14,900 --> 00:22:17,802
I expected you
might come, Captain.

393
00:22:17,870 --> 00:22:19,031
HARVEY: My God.

394
00:22:21,207 --> 00:22:22,869
There's two of them.

395
00:22:33,986 --> 00:22:36,979
May I put my hands
down now, gentlemen?

396
00:22:37,056 --> 00:22:38,354
Please?

397
00:22:38,891 --> 00:22:41,690
Apologies, Mr. Wilde,
you took us by surprise.

398
00:22:43,462 --> 00:22:46,330
It's understandable.
You can call me Jeremiah.

399
00:22:46,899 --> 00:22:48,993
That's name my mother gave me.

400
00:22:49,635 --> 00:22:50,864
You solved her murder,

401
00:22:50,970 --> 00:22:53,565
and Captain Gordon, I owe
you a debt of gratitude for that,

402
00:22:53,672 --> 00:22:57,074
and for putting Jerome in
Arkham, where he belongs.

403
00:22:59,645 --> 00:23:01,637
He never mentioned you before.

404
00:23:03,549 --> 00:23:07,042
No, I suppose you wouldn't know
from the letters my mother sent me,

405
00:23:07,119 --> 00:23:09,179
he never spoke
of me after I left.

406
00:23:09,722 --> 00:23:11,088
Left where?

407
00:23:12,725 --> 00:23:13,886
The Circus.

408
00:23:15,227 --> 00:23:17,594
They hid me away to
protect me from him.

409
00:23:17,897 --> 00:23:21,231
See, we are always
different, Jerome and I.

410
00:23:21,467 --> 00:23:24,562
From an early age, I showed a
proficiency for maths and design

411
00:23:24,637 --> 00:23:27,129
and Jerome, mainly the
mutilation of alley cats.

412
00:23:27,239 --> 00:23:30,368
On my 10th birthday, he
held a cake knife to my throat.

413
00:23:30,709 --> 00:23:32,769
A few weeks later,
he lit my bed on fire.

414
00:23:32,878 --> 00:23:34,710
It was like living
in a nightmare.

415
00:23:35,047 --> 00:23:37,846
My mother knew, eventually,
one day he would succeed, so...

416
00:23:38,384 --> 00:23:41,718
One night, my uncle came
to my room while Jerome slept

417
00:23:42,221 --> 00:23:43,917
and told me that he
was taking me away.

418
00:23:44,757 --> 00:23:46,191
I had no idea where,

419
00:23:46,892 --> 00:23:50,021
but I kissed my mother goodbye,

420
00:23:50,396 --> 00:23:54,026
told her I love her, and
I never saw her again.

421
00:23:55,868 --> 00:23:57,700
He took you to St. Ignatius.

422
00:23:59,104 --> 00:24:03,599
Got a new name, new life, where I
was finally able to live without fear.

423
00:24:04,143 --> 00:24:06,271
But in my heart,

424
00:24:07,713 --> 00:24:09,313
I knew one day Jerome
would come for me.

425
00:24:09,982 --> 00:24:11,780
Well, that day is today, pops.

426
00:24:12,451 --> 00:24:14,977
GORDON: We have reason to
believe Jerome knows where you are.

427
00:24:15,087 --> 00:24:16,646
We need to move
you to a safe location

428
00:24:16,755 --> 00:24:17,984
until we can apprehend him.

429
00:24:18,090 --> 00:24:19,524
That won't be
necessary, gentlemen.

430
00:24:19,625 --> 00:24:22,065
I've spent the bulk of my life
preparing for this eventuality.

431
00:24:22,127 --> 00:24:24,528
I am safest here in my home.

432
00:24:24,663 --> 00:24:27,929
Not anymore. Jerome
knows the name of your proxy.

433
00:24:28,000 --> 00:24:30,299
He got it from your boss
just before he executed him.

434
00:24:30,602 --> 00:24:32,969
I heard about Jerome
visiting Alan Hayes.

435
00:24:33,539 --> 00:24:34,768
It's tragic.

436
00:24:35,708 --> 00:24:38,803
But the proxy's name I
gave to Alan was a fake.

437
00:24:45,451 --> 00:24:46,475
You're lying.

438
00:24:47,853 --> 00:24:49,253
I beg your pardon?

439
00:24:50,689 --> 00:24:53,284
There's something
about this guy that's fishy.

440
00:24:55,427 --> 00:24:59,330
Security cameras
outside were turned on,

441
00:24:59,631 --> 00:25:01,293
so why are the
monitors in here off?

442
00:25:02,001 --> 00:25:03,799
I don't see why that
is any of your concern.

443
00:25:03,869 --> 00:25:05,838
Now, if you'll excuse
me, I'm a very busy man.

444
00:25:05,938 --> 00:25:07,270
Yeah, I'm sure that you are.

445
00:25:07,339 --> 00:25:10,070
Please don't touch that.
That's very sensitive equipment.

446
00:25:13,078 --> 00:25:15,411
(SCOFFS)

447
00:25:16,482 --> 00:25:19,213
(GRUNTING)

448
00:25:28,694 --> 00:25:32,597
Oh, yeah. I'd say that's
pretty damn sensitive.

449
00:25:35,434 --> 00:25:39,496
Ladies and gentlemen, a gauntlet
has been laid at the Riddler's feet.

450
00:25:40,005 --> 00:25:42,804
Our leader, Doc Thompkins,

451
00:25:43,075 --> 00:25:45,840
she thinks that she
knows what's good for you.

452
00:25:46,245 --> 00:25:48,976
She thinks stapling a live
barracuda to someone's face

453
00:25:49,048 --> 00:25:50,880
isn't damn good entertainment.

454
00:25:50,983 --> 00:25:52,975
(CROWD BOOING)

455
00:25:54,420 --> 00:25:55,683
Just get on with the show, Ed.

456
00:25:55,754 --> 00:25:57,985
Okay, enough! What time is it?

457
00:25:58,057 --> 00:25:59,081
ALL: Riddle time!

458
00:25:59,191 --> 00:26:00,386
What time is it?

459
00:26:00,492 --> 00:26:01,687
ALL: Riddle time!

460
00:26:01,760 --> 00:26:03,023
Lila!

461
00:26:03,095 --> 00:26:05,326
(CROWD CHEERING)

462
00:26:06,432 --> 00:26:07,491
Prepare the timer.

463
00:26:13,472 --> 00:26:14,872
I can be broken

464
00:26:15,974 --> 00:26:17,374
without being held.

465
00:26:17,443 --> 00:26:20,072
Given, and then taken away.

466
00:26:20,312 --> 00:26:22,907
Some people use me to deceive,

467
00:26:22,981 --> 00:26:26,440
but when delivered, I
am the greatest gift of all.

468
00:26:26,552 --> 00:26:27,952
What am I?

469
00:26:52,444 --> 00:26:53,810
You stumped?

470
00:26:54,279 --> 00:26:57,113
Of course I am.
You're the Riddler.

471
00:26:58,217 --> 00:26:59,217
So, you give up?

472
00:26:59,885 --> 00:27:01,046
LEE: No.

473
00:27:02,087 --> 00:27:03,453
You're gonna give me the answer.

474
00:27:03,555 --> 00:27:05,990
(LAUGHS)

475
00:27:07,059 --> 00:27:08,288
Really? How's that?

476
00:27:09,761 --> 00:27:11,662
Because deep down, you want to.

477
00:27:14,733 --> 00:27:18,431
So, that's your plan. To
appeal to the inner Ed.

478
00:27:19,505 --> 00:27:20,973
You played the wrong hand, Doc.

479
00:27:21,373 --> 00:27:22,773
Ed is gone,

480
00:27:22,841 --> 00:27:26,107
and I will not be manipulated
by you the way that dope was.

481
00:27:26,645 --> 00:27:28,341
I didn't manipulate
him, I helped him.

482
00:27:28,447 --> 00:27:31,178
No. No, you lied to him.

483
00:27:31,517 --> 00:27:33,679
You promised to fix him.

484
00:27:33,886 --> 00:27:36,481
And that pathetic
dust mite believed you,

485
00:27:36,622 --> 00:27:39,114
causing me to be locked
inside of his feeble mind.

486
00:27:40,359 --> 00:27:44,160
Okay, you have 10 seconds
left to find your answer!

487
00:27:45,564 --> 00:27:46,998
So, prepare to lose.

488
00:27:47,099 --> 00:27:48,465
But I know the answer now.

489
00:27:48,534 --> 00:27:49,661
(SCOFFS)

490
00:27:50,836 --> 00:27:51,929
You just gave it to me.

491
00:27:54,673 --> 00:27:56,005
What are you talking about?

492
00:27:56,108 --> 00:27:59,704
You're right. I told Ed I
would fix him, and I didn't.

493
00:28:00,012 --> 00:28:01,344
I broke my promise.

494
00:28:02,447 --> 00:28:04,439
And that, Riddler,

495
00:28:07,119 --> 00:28:08,178
is the answer.

496
00:28:08,887 --> 00:28:10,014
A promise.

497
00:28:21,867 --> 00:28:24,496
(ALL CHEERING)

498
00:28:27,673 --> 00:28:29,107
Well played.

499
00:28:32,077 --> 00:28:33,602
We're only halfway through.

500
00:28:34,546 --> 00:28:37,744
And I assure you,
you will not stump me.

501
00:28:42,187 --> 00:28:45,282
Boo. (LAUGHING)

502
00:28:47,092 --> 00:28:48,651
You need to take
us to your brother.

503
00:28:50,429 --> 00:28:51,658
I can't do that.

504
00:28:52,364 --> 00:28:54,094
What if he's nuttier
than the other one?

505
00:28:54,199 --> 00:28:55,531
I'm nothing like Jerome!

506
00:28:55,601 --> 00:28:57,593
Do you want me to put
you in a cell next to his?

507
00:28:57,703 --> 00:28:59,223
Because that is
where this is heading.

508
00:29:00,005 --> 00:29:01,496
And then what, Captain Gordon?

509
00:29:02,107 --> 00:29:05,407
You couldn't hold him,
Arkham couldn't, I can.

510
00:29:05,677 --> 00:29:08,044
And I will make sure
Jerome never escapes again.

511
00:29:08,113 --> 00:29:10,275
- No, no, no. Jerome comes with us.
- (ALARM BEEPING)

512
00:29:10,916 --> 00:29:12,716
And if you wanna make
this easier on yourself,

513
00:29:12,751 --> 00:29:14,583
I suggest you stay
the hell out of the way.

514
00:29:14,686 --> 00:29:15,949
Whoa, whoa, whoa!

515
00:29:16,121 --> 00:29:17,612
The loonies are outside.

516
00:29:21,493 --> 00:29:22,825
No, no.

517
00:29:25,097 --> 00:29:26,258
How'd they find me?

518
00:29:26,665 --> 00:29:29,328
Must've followed Jerome.
We need to move. Now.

519
00:29:31,737 --> 00:29:33,729
(ALARM BLARING)

520
00:29:34,273 --> 00:29:35,764
This is not good.

521
00:29:35,941 --> 00:29:37,273
Is there another
way out of here?

522
00:29:37,342 --> 00:29:39,243
Yes, but I'm not leaving Echo.

523
00:29:39,311 --> 00:29:41,507
The loonies will
use her against us.

524
00:29:41,613 --> 00:29:43,275
She's devoted her life to me.

525
00:29:43,348 --> 00:29:45,442
We get you out first and
we can come back for her.

526
00:29:45,817 --> 00:29:46,944
Deal?

527
00:29:48,353 --> 00:29:50,033
As a child, I was
obsessed with labyrinths,

528
00:29:51,056 --> 00:29:52,336
so I designed my house like one.

529
00:29:52,891 --> 00:29:54,902
- You mean this place is a maze?
- Easy enough to get in.

530
00:29:54,926 --> 00:29:56,792
I'm the only one who
knows the way out.

531
00:29:57,329 --> 00:29:58,490
Stay close to me.

532
00:30:06,505 --> 00:30:09,771
The maze is built on a hundred
acres. There are 73 dead ends.

533
00:30:10,108 --> 00:30:13,010
I once invited the top maze designers
in the country to try to solve it.

534
00:30:13,412 --> 00:30:16,143
One did. It took him three days.

535
00:30:16,248 --> 00:30:17,511
GORDON: And how much further?

536
00:30:17,616 --> 00:30:19,676
Not far. We just passed
the final switchback point.

537
00:30:20,452 --> 00:30:22,318
If I knew this thing
was a hundred acres,

538
00:30:22,387 --> 00:30:24,686
I would have stopped and
got a sandwich before we came.

539
00:30:28,360 --> 00:30:29,360
Echo.

540
00:30:30,195 --> 00:30:33,495
- Thank God you're okay.
- Hey, hey, hey. She's hypnotized.

541
00:30:33,565 --> 00:30:34,565
(GROANS)

542
00:30:38,003 --> 00:30:39,835
(ALL GRUNTING)

543
00:30:50,749 --> 00:30:52,718
Echo. It's me.

544
00:30:53,118 --> 00:30:54,552
It's me. Ow!

545
00:30:55,454 --> 00:30:57,150
Gordon! Help!

546
00:30:57,889 --> 00:30:58,948
Gordon!

547
00:31:00,659 --> 00:31:02,127
She's a pistol.

548
00:31:02,194 --> 00:31:03,457
Come on.

549
00:31:06,965 --> 00:31:09,662
Echo, please. Where
are you taking me?

550
00:31:09,735 --> 00:31:12,136
- I'm taking you to Jerome.
- No.

551
00:31:12,204 --> 00:31:14,400
He'll do horrible
things to me. Echo!

552
00:31:14,506 --> 00:31:15,701
Stop!

553
00:31:17,743 --> 00:31:19,006
Let him go.

554
00:31:20,412 --> 00:31:22,005
(ALL GRUNT)

555
00:31:27,018 --> 00:31:28,418
(GROANS)

556
00:31:30,689 --> 00:31:31,884
Thanks.

557
00:31:31,957 --> 00:31:34,222
- He went that way.
- All right.

558
00:31:36,461 --> 00:31:37,690
Jeremiah?

559
00:31:41,433 --> 00:31:42,867
Let's try this way.

560
00:31:48,707 --> 00:31:49,902
(SIGHS)

561
00:31:50,275 --> 00:31:52,039
JEROME: Hello, brother.

562
00:31:58,250 --> 00:31:59,250
(GASPS)

563
00:31:59,351 --> 00:32:02,446
So, how you been?
Oh, you look great.

564
00:32:02,554 --> 00:32:04,022
(LAUGHING)

565
00:32:04,289 --> 00:32:06,849
To think I used to be
the handsome one, right?

566
00:32:08,226 --> 00:32:09,922
How'd you find your
way through the maze?

567
00:32:10,028 --> 00:32:14,124
Oh, bro, we might not
look the same anymore,

568
00:32:14,232 --> 00:32:17,031
but we still think the same.

569
00:32:17,102 --> 00:32:19,799
Plus, you used to draw those
stupid things all the time as a kid.

570
00:32:20,138 --> 00:32:22,039
I paid attention.

571
00:32:22,207 --> 00:32:24,108
Hey, Hat Head,

572
00:32:24,209 --> 00:32:27,737
take Crane, find Jimbo and
his bearded sidekick. Kill them.

573
00:32:31,283 --> 00:32:35,277
I've been waiting for
this moment for 15 years,

574
00:32:35,487 --> 00:32:39,982
ever since you ran away in the
middle of the night like a coward.

575
00:32:40,091 --> 00:32:41,684
- You're insane.
- Yeah.

576
00:32:41,793 --> 00:32:44,194
And I tried telling Mom, but
she didn't want to listen to me.

577
00:32:45,831 --> 00:32:48,075
You blame me for everything
that's gone wrong in your life,

578
00:32:48,099 --> 00:32:50,364
but the truth is, Jerome,

579
00:32:51,870 --> 00:32:53,270
you were born bad.

580
00:32:55,974 --> 00:32:57,533
Born bad, huh?

581
00:32:58,610 --> 00:33:03,412
So, that's why you made her
think that I tried to kill you, right?

582
00:33:03,482 --> 00:33:05,815
- Yeah, we both...
- What was it again? What was it?

583
00:33:05,917 --> 00:33:08,443
I put a blade to your
throat. No, no, no.

584
00:33:08,520 --> 00:33:11,319
- I tried to light you on fire.
- We both know you wanted to.

585
00:33:11,423 --> 00:33:13,289
Yeah, that was a
funny story, wasn't it?

586
00:33:15,427 --> 00:33:19,091
Okay, maybe it didn't
happen exactly like that,

587
00:33:20,198 --> 00:33:22,258
but I didn't have a
choice and I was right.

588
00:33:22,701 --> 00:33:23,862
Huh?

589
00:33:25,971 --> 00:33:27,530
You killed our mother.

590
00:33:30,408 --> 00:33:32,343
She did deserve it though.

591
00:33:34,513 --> 00:33:38,848
After that whore hid you
away, she gave up on me,

592
00:33:39,851 --> 00:33:42,013
poisoned by your stories.

593
00:33:42,153 --> 00:33:44,713
You turned everyone I
ever loved against me.

594
00:33:44,823 --> 00:33:46,553
My own flesh and blood!

595
00:33:48,059 --> 00:33:50,324
I guess it's like what they say,

596
00:33:51,029 --> 00:33:54,363
"We all could go insane
with just one bad day."

597
00:33:55,767 --> 00:33:59,033
I guess with you it's
more like one bad spray.

598
00:34:00,038 --> 00:34:01,199
You'll see.

599
00:34:01,306 --> 00:34:02,740
(LAUGHING)

600
00:34:06,678 --> 00:34:08,478
- Listen to what I have to say.
- Oh, hell no!

601
00:34:08,513 --> 00:34:10,641
You are not
hypnotizing me again!

602
00:34:10,815 --> 00:34:13,410
(YELLING)

603
00:34:13,518 --> 00:34:14,679
That's one way to do it.

604
00:34:14,753 --> 00:34:17,917
Must go! Must go!
Must go! Must go!

605
00:34:23,161 --> 00:34:24,390
(EXCLAIMS)

606
00:34:24,596 --> 00:34:26,326
What are you gonna
do to me, Jerome?

607
00:34:26,431 --> 00:34:29,731
Come on. I'm gonna
kill you, of course.

608
00:34:30,101 --> 00:34:31,364
But first...

609
00:34:33,071 --> 00:34:35,472
I'm gonna drive you mad.

610
00:34:36,041 --> 00:34:37,481
But don't worry,
you won't be alone.

611
00:34:37,943 --> 00:34:40,071
All of Gotham will
be joining you, too.

612
00:34:40,445 --> 00:34:44,041
Must go! Must go! They're
after me and the Scarecrow!

613
00:34:44,115 --> 00:34:45,640
These guys just
don't give up, huh?

614
00:34:45,750 --> 00:34:47,378
(HARVEY YELLING)

615
00:34:48,653 --> 00:34:49,653
(GASPS)

616
00:34:49,754 --> 00:34:51,382
Get any closer,
and I splatter him!

617
00:34:51,456 --> 00:34:53,084
I'm gonna splatter broski.

618
00:34:53,592 --> 00:34:56,619
No, you won't. If you wanted him
dead you would've killed him already.

619
00:34:56,728 --> 00:34:59,562
(GIGGLES) He's
right. I'll see you soon.

620
00:34:59,631 --> 00:35:00,631
- Mwah.
- (YELPS)

621
00:35:03,902 --> 00:35:05,803
(JEROME LAUGHING MANIACALLY)

622
00:35:18,283 --> 00:35:19,717
Okay, Doc.

623
00:35:20,452 --> 00:35:22,172
You've had enough
time to think of a riddle.

624
00:35:22,821 --> 00:35:24,341
I hope you've come
up with a good one.

625
00:35:25,323 --> 00:35:26,416
I have.

626
00:35:26,491 --> 00:35:27,618
Then ask away.

627
00:35:40,839 --> 00:35:43,104
What three words
are said too much,

628
00:35:44,643 --> 00:35:48,045
meant by few, but wanted by all?

629
00:35:48,380 --> 00:35:49,678
(SCOFFS) That's your riddle?

630
00:35:51,683 --> 00:35:53,276
You're kidding, right?

631
00:35:55,620 --> 00:35:56,883
What's the answer?

632
00:35:56,988 --> 00:35:58,889
Easy. I love...

633
00:35:59,391 --> 00:36:00,391
(CLEARS THROAT)

634
00:36:04,462 --> 00:36:05,623
(NYGMA SIGHS)

635
00:36:07,632 --> 00:36:10,101
The answer is, I love...

636
00:36:10,502 --> 00:36:11,868
I love what, Ed?

637
00:36:12,103 --> 00:36:14,265
- That's not my name.
- Then what's the answer?

638
00:36:14,339 --> 00:36:15,339
You tricked me.

639
00:36:16,641 --> 00:36:18,837
You're trying to awaken
a love that isn't there.

640
00:36:18,943 --> 00:36:21,845
If it isn't there,
then say the words.

641
00:36:34,059 --> 00:36:35,493
I won't say it.

642
00:36:35,560 --> 00:36:37,552
(CROWD BOOING)

643
00:36:37,662 --> 00:36:38,857
MAN: Come on.

644
00:36:39,364 --> 00:36:40,388
Say it.

645
00:36:42,867 --> 00:36:44,096
Time's up.

646
00:36:46,204 --> 00:36:48,730
(CROWD CHEERING)

647
00:37:21,406 --> 00:37:22,430
Thank you.

648
00:37:22,841 --> 00:37:24,104
What did I say?

649
00:37:24,442 --> 00:37:26,274
(YELPING)

650
00:37:27,212 --> 00:37:29,238
I can help you get Tabitha back!

651
00:37:30,215 --> 00:37:32,548
I know how to
reverse your condition.

652
00:37:34,853 --> 00:37:35,912
(GASPING)

653
00:37:36,020 --> 00:37:37,181
Talk.

654
00:37:37,255 --> 00:37:39,486
This barrel is from
Slaughter Swamp.

655
00:37:40,125 --> 00:37:41,957
It turned you into what you are.

656
00:37:42,994 --> 00:37:44,087
Talk.

657
00:37:44,162 --> 00:37:47,132
Professor Hugo Strange is
the one man who can cure you.

658
00:37:47,999 --> 00:37:50,662
Join me, and we will find him.

659
00:37:52,570 --> 00:37:54,061
(COUGHING)

660
00:37:59,811 --> 00:38:02,076
I thought I was going to
be stuck like this forever.

661
00:38:03,782 --> 00:38:06,183
You don't have to be, friend.

662
00:38:12,123 --> 00:38:14,786
Okay. I'll join you.

663
00:38:15,727 --> 00:38:17,559
But you'd better deliver.

664
00:38:20,565 --> 00:38:21,931
And I'm not your friend.

665
00:38:25,303 --> 00:38:26,999
I can live with those terms.

666
00:38:45,857 --> 00:38:47,382
(FOOTSTEPS APPROACHING)

667
00:38:54,332 --> 00:38:56,824
Why did you open your
club in The Narrows?

668
00:38:59,037 --> 00:39:01,302
You could've set up
shop anywhere in Gotham.

669
00:39:03,041 --> 00:39:04,202
You wanted my attention.

670
00:39:04,509 --> 00:39:06,978
I came back here because
the people love me.

671
00:39:07,045 --> 00:39:09,071
People didn't come here
because they love you, Ed,

672
00:39:09,180 --> 00:39:10,546
they came for the money.

673
00:39:12,150 --> 00:39:15,609
But I can give them what
they want. I just need your help.

674
00:39:16,955 --> 00:39:18,321
Why would I help you?

675
00:39:20,558 --> 00:39:23,153
Because I know
what you really want.

676
00:39:25,697 --> 00:39:26,960
You love me.

677
00:39:29,033 --> 00:39:30,365
You've loved me all along.

678
00:39:32,871 --> 00:39:34,237
You're wrong.

679
00:39:34,873 --> 00:39:36,239
That was Ed.

680
00:39:38,910 --> 00:39:40,037
Okay.

681
00:39:40,845 --> 00:39:42,040
So, do it.

682
00:39:44,048 --> 00:39:45,380
Kill me.

683
00:39:45,450 --> 00:39:46,543
(GASPS)

684
00:39:56,461 --> 00:39:59,898
What's your record for how many
banks you've robbed in one day?

685
00:40:02,467 --> 00:40:03,935
Three. Why?

686
00:40:04,569 --> 00:40:05,969
I think you can do better.

687
00:40:06,971 --> 00:40:08,769
You're playing a
dangerous game, Lee.

688
00:40:09,440 --> 00:40:10,440
I know.

689
00:40:26,791 --> 00:40:28,851
(INDISTINCT CHATTER)

690
00:40:38,236 --> 00:40:39,795
It's all right. Come on.

691
00:40:46,611 --> 00:40:48,477
- Captain.
- It's okay.

692
00:40:48,780 --> 00:40:51,807
This is Jeremiah Valeska,
Jerome's twin brother.

693
00:40:52,784 --> 00:40:54,514
We need to put him
into protective custody.

694
00:40:54,852 --> 00:40:56,787
Okay. Follow me.

695
00:41:00,625 --> 00:41:01,649
Thank you.

696
00:41:02,827 --> 00:41:06,764
Captain Gordon, I wouldn't
be alive if it weren't for you.

697
00:41:11,836 --> 00:41:12,997
(SIGHS)

698
00:41:15,306 --> 00:41:16,569
Hell of a day.

699
00:41:18,009 --> 00:41:19,170
What are you thinking?

700
00:41:19,811 --> 00:41:23,077
GORDON: Jerome told his brother
his plan was to turn Gotham mad.

701
00:41:23,982 --> 00:41:25,917
How the hell is he
going to do that, Harvey?

702
00:41:25,984 --> 00:41:29,113
I don't know. I
thought it already was.

703
00:41:36,027 --> 00:41:37,359
(WHIRRING)

704
00:41:38,529 --> 00:41:39,792
- (GUN CLICKS)
- (GRUNTS)

705
00:41:41,132 --> 00:41:42,361
(WHIRRING)

706
00:41:42,467 --> 00:41:43,467
(GUN CLICKS)

707
00:41:44,168 --> 00:41:45,261
Damn.

708
00:41:49,073 --> 00:41:50,371
(GUNSHOT)

709
00:41:50,708 --> 00:41:52,074
(THUDS)

710
00:41:52,243 --> 00:41:54,303
Doesn't even make
me feel better at all.

711
00:41:54,379 --> 00:41:55,403
CRANE: Jerome.

712
00:41:56,314 --> 00:41:59,045
The test subject has arrived.

713
00:42:01,085 --> 00:42:02,576
Hmm.

714
00:42:02,687 --> 00:42:04,849
A little large for a test
subject, don't you think?

715
00:42:04,922 --> 00:42:08,188
He is not your test
subject. This is Grundy.

716
00:42:08,559 --> 00:42:10,892
He will make a welcome
addition to the team.

717
00:42:11,095 --> 00:42:13,360
Mmm-hmm. Mmm-hmm.

718
00:42:14,432 --> 00:42:16,424
I like him. He's scary.

719
00:42:17,035 --> 00:42:19,971
Good thinking, Mr. Oswald.
Nice to meet you, big fellow.

720
00:42:20,972 --> 00:42:22,065
Ow!

721
00:42:22,874 --> 00:42:24,035
Ah!

722
00:42:24,342 --> 00:42:26,334
- Test subject?
- Here to test your plan,

723
00:42:26,577 --> 00:42:28,136
an unwilling businessman.

724
00:42:28,713 --> 00:42:31,239
MAN: No, no, no! Please, please.

725
00:42:31,883 --> 00:42:33,215
Please don't hurt me.

726
00:42:33,317 --> 00:42:36,879
CRANE: Oh, this
won't hurt a bit.

727
00:42:38,322 --> 00:42:39,585
(MAN GROANS)

728
00:42:40,892 --> 00:42:44,329
(MAN COUGHING)

729
00:42:49,067 --> 00:42:51,798
(MAN LAUGHING MANIACALLY)

730
00:43:11,389 --> 00:43:13,381
(BOTH LAUGHING)

731
00:43:13,458 --> 00:43:16,053
Oh, we're gonna
need a lot more of this!

732
00:43:16,127 --> 00:43:18,096
(BOTH CONTINUE LAUGHING)

733
00:43:19,931 --> 00:43:21,126
(SCREAMING)

734
00:44:01,539 --> 00:44:02,539
English-US-SDH
